  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Write ${x}^{2}+36$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{2}+36=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+36 & ={x}^{2}-(-36) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+6i)(x-6i)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
